<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">An O’Nan configuration in a unital is a set of four lines forming a quadrilateral, where the six intersections of pairs of lines are points of the unital. In 2019 Feng and Li <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> elegantly construct O’Nan configurations in orthogonal and Tits Buekenhout-Metz unitals. We extend their work by extending their construction to a Fano plane embedded in the orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unital of even order. We deduce that there exist O’Nan configurations in orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unitals different to those of Feng and Li, and make a conjecture about Fano planes embedded in orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unitals.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p">AMS code: 51E20
<br class="ltx_break">Keywords: unital, Buekenhout-Metz unital, O’Nan configuration</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unital of order <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></em> is a 2–<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n^{3}+1,n+1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n^{3}+1,n+1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design. Unitals of prime power order <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> exist in the Desarguesian projective planes <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, as the absolute points and lines of a hermitian polarity. Such unitals are called <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classical</em> unitals. Buekenhout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> constructed unitals in 2–dimensional translation planes. This was generalised by Metz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to construct non-classical unitals in <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. These unitals are called Buekenhout-Metz (BM) unitals. They have the property that every line of <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q)</annotation></semantics></math> meets the unital in 1 or <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+1</annotation></semantics></math> points. Further, the unital has exactly one point on the line at infinity, called the <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">special</em> point. For a comprehensive treatise on unitals, see Barwick and Ebert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">O’Nan configuration</em> in a unital is a set of four distinct lines forming a quadrilateral, whose six points of intersection lie on the unital. In 1972, O’Nan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> noted that these configurations do not occur in the classical unital. It was conjectured by Piper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> that this property characterizes the classical unital within the class of all unitals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In 2019, Feng and Li <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> showed the existence of O’Nan configurations in orthogonal BM unitals in <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, for both <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> even and odd. They also show the existence of O’Nan configurations in the Buekenhout-Tits unital.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we use their results to show the existence of Fano planes in BM unitals of even order, which supports the following conjecture:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#59DEFF;">Conjecture</span><span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Every non-classical unital <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a Fano plane if <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Buekenhout-Metz unitals</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Field properties for even <math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give properties of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is an even prime power.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In general, consider a prime <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and a positive integer <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=p^{m}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=p^{m}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is any divisor of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, then the trace function from <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{p^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{p^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Tr}_{p^{m}/p^{d}}(x)=x+x^{p^{d}}+x^{p^{d+1}}+\cdots+x^{p^{m-d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Tr</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Tr}_{p^{m}/p^{d}}(x)=x+x^{p^{d}}+x^{p^{d+1}}+\cdots+x^{p^{m-d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose now that <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2^{m}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2^{m}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">absolute trace</em> of an element <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{Tr}_{2^{m}/2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Tr</mtext><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{Tr}_{2^{m}/2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{0},\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{0},\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of elements of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> with absolute trace 0 and 1 respectively. From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Lemma 4.21]</cite>, we can choose <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\backslash\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\backslash\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{q}=\delta+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{q}=\delta+1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{2}=\delta+v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{2}=\delta+v</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{T}_{1},v\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{T}_{1},v\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For any element <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> we will also use the notation</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{x}=x+x^{q}=\mbox{Tr}_{q^{2}/q}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><menclose notation="box"><mi>x</mi></menclose><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Tr</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{x}=x+x^{q}=\mbox{Tr}_{q^{2}/q}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=k_{0}+k_{1}\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=k_{0}+k_{1}\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0},k_{1}\in\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0},k_{1}\in\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> it is easy to calculate <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{q}=(k_{0}+k_{1})+k_{1}\delta=k+k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{q}=(k_{0}+k_{1})+k_{1}\delta=k+k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{q+1}=k^{q}k=k_{0}^{2}+k_{0}k_{1}+vk_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{q+1}=k^{q}k=k_{0}^{2}+k_{0}k_{1}+vk_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{k}=k+k^{q}=k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><menclose notation="box"><mi>k</mi></menclose><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{k}=k+k^{q}=k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{k\delta}=(k\delta)+(k\delta)^{q}=k_{0}+k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></menclose><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{k\delta}=(k\delta)+(k\delta)^{q}=k_{0}+k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{k\delta^{q}}=(k\delta^{q})+(k^{q}\delta)=k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></menclose><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{k\delta^{q}}=(k\delta^{q})+(k^{q}\delta)=k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Coordinates of the orthogonal BM unitals</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ebert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> showed that an orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unital <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is projectively equivalent to the following set of points</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}=\{(x,ax^{2}+bx^{q+1}+r,1)\bigm|r\in\mathbb{F}_{q},\ x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\}\cup\{T_{\infty}=(0,1,0)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}=\{(x,ax^{2}+bx^{q+1}+r,1)\bigm|r\in\mathbb{F}_{q},\ x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\}\cup\{T_{\infty}=(0,1,0)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the discriminant <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\frac{a^{q+1}}{(b^{q}+b)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\frac{a^{q+1}}{(b^{q}+b)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of elements of <em id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">absolute trace</em> 0 of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Further <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> is classical if and only if <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, an affine point <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(x,y,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(x,y,1)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ax^{2}+a^{q}x^{2q}+(b+b^{q})x^{q+1}+y+y^{q}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ax^{2}+a^{q}x^{2q}+(b+b^{q})x^{q+1}+y+y^{q}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>The automorphism group of the orthogonal BM unital, even <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This was investigated by Ebert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> but we use the notation from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 4.23]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We assume that the BM unital <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> is not classical, that is, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">F</span> be the smallest subfield of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> containing the discriminant <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the subgroup of the automorphism group <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{P}\Gamma{\textup{L}}(3,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>P</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>L</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{P}\Gamma{\textup{L}}(3,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> leaving <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> invariant. Then <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> also fixes <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The order of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mq^{3}(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mq^{3}(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">F</span>. <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has four point orbits, namely, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{T_{\infty}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{\infty}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}\backslash T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}\backslash T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}\backslash T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}\backslash T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})\backslash({\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}\cup\ell_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})\backslash({\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}\cup\ell_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>The equivalence of orthogonal BM unitals, even <math id="S2.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From Ebert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, two BM unitals <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent if and only if we can find <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> a field automorphism of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(a^{\tau}\gamma^{2}v,\ \ b^{\tau}\gamma^{q+1}v+u)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(a^{\tau}\gamma^{2}v,\ \ b^{\tau}\gamma^{q+1}v+u).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of inequivalent orthogonal BM unitals in <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2,3,4,5,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2,3,4,5,6</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,2,4,4,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,2,4,4,8</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively, one of which is the classical unital <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 4.14]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For even <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, if  <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> is any non-classical orthogonal BM unital, then without loss of generality we may assume</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">From above, given <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(a^{\tau}\gamma^{2}\nu,\ \ b^{\tau}\gamma^{q+1}+u)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>ν</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(a^{\tau}\gamma^{2}\nu,\ \ b^{\tau}\gamma^{q+1}+u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">is an equivalent unital if <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For Part 1, we take <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=</annotation></semantics></math> identity, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1/\sqrt{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1/\sqrt{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=0</annotation></semantics></math> to get <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,b/(\sqrt{a})^{q+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>a</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,b/(\sqrt{a})^{q+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For Part 2, the proof is in Feng and Li <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Section 3.2]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Fano planes in Buekenhout-Metz unitals</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well known that no O’Nan configuration contains the special (infinite) point <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}=(0,1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}=(0,1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> of a Buekenhout-Metz unital <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Lemma 7.42]</cite>. Hence it follows there is no Fano plane containing the special point <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it is possible that a line of the Fano plane, regarded as a line of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, contains <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> even, consider a BM unital <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> with special point <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> at infinity.
We say a Fano plane in a BM unital embedded in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with special point <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">BM-special Fano plane</em> if one of the lines of the Fano plane, when considered as a line as of <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, contains the special point <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>;
Otherwise, we call it a <em id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">BM-ordinary Fano plane</em>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>Contribution of this paper</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Feng and Li <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> have three beautiful constructions of O’Nan configurations. In this paper we consider their O’Nan configurations in the orthogonal case for even <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Their O’Nan configurations have a line which contains the special point <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by showing that the O’Nan configuration of Feng and Li can be extended to a Fano plane.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The O’Nan configuration of Feng and Li <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for orthogonal BM unitals of even order, can be extended to a Fano plane contained within the BM unital, that is, to a BM-special Fano plane.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We further investigate the BM-special Fano planes, and prove the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2^{m}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2^{m}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{1}(1,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{1}(1,b)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{2}(1,b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{2}(1,b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be any two non-classical orthogonal BM unitals of <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the number of BM-special Fano planes in <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the same.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=4,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=4,8</annotation></semantics></math> there is only one equivalence class of non-classical unitals, so the above theorem holds trivially.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since a Fano plane contains seven O’Nan configurations we have the following Corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2^{m}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2^{m}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. In orthogonal BM unitals of <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist O’Nan configurations, where none of the four lines of the configuration is incident with the special point <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> of the BM unital.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main results for BM-unitals, even <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Existence of Fano planes in even order orthogonal BM unitals</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 2.6 Contribution of this paper ‣ 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals ‣ A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Feng and Li choose <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (see Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 2.4 The equivalence of orthogonal BM unitals, even 𝑞 ‣ 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals ‣ A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(2)), and define</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})\rightarrow{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}),\quad\phi\colon(x,y,z)\mapsto(x^{q},y^{q},z^{q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext>PG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})\rightarrow{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}),\quad\phi\colon(x,y,z)\mapsto(x^{q},y^{q},z^{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which stabilises <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, fixing the line <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\infty}=\{(0,r,1)\colon r\in\mathbb{F}_{q}\}\cup\{(0,1,0)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\infty}=\{(0,r,1)\colon r\in\mathbb{F}_{q}\}\cup\{(0,1,0)\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, and no other point of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>. They show there exists points <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,R\in{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,R\in{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,R\in[\delta,1,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,R\in[\delta,1,0]</annotation></semantics></math>, and that if <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(0,0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(0,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the lines</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QR^{\phi},RQ^{\phi},QQ^{\phi},RR^{\phi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QR^{\phi},RQ^{\phi},QQ^{\phi},RR^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">are four lines with the six points of intersection <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q,R,Q^{\phi},R^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q,R,Q^{\phi},R^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=QQ^{\phi}\cap RR^{\phi}\in B_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=QQ^{\phi}\cap RR^{\phi}\in B_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> lying on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence forming an O’Nan configuration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that this completes to a Fano plane.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=QR\cap Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=QR\cap Q^{\phi}R^{\phi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">so <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\phi}=Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}\cap QR=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\phi}=Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}\cap QR=N</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed point. As <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is even, note that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,M,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,M,N</annotation></semantics></math> are collinear, and as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PM</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, and as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the fixed points on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,0,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,0,0]</annotation></semantics></math> it follows that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(0,s,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(0,s,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathbb{F}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathbb{F}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the six lines</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QR^{\phi},RQ^{\phi},QQ^{\phi},RR^{\phi},QR,Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QR^{\phi},RQ^{\phi},QQ^{\phi},RR^{\phi},QR,Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and the seven points</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q,R,Q^{\phi},R^{\phi},M,N" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q,R,Q^{\phi},R^{\phi},M,N</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">all belong to <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so form a Fano plane, completing the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 2.6 Contribution of this paper ‣ 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals ‣ A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Number of BM-special Fano planes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that the number of BM-special Fano planes in orthogonal BM-unitals of even order depends only the order of the unital.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now calculate the general form of a BM-special Fano plane. By Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 The automorphism group of the orthogonal BM unital, even 𝑞 ‣ 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals ‣ A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, we may assume that the point <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=(0,0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=(0,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the Fano plane. As the Fano plane is BM-special, there are a further two points on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VT_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VT_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, without loss of generality let these be <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(0,s,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(0,s,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(0,t,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(0,t,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq t</annotation></semantics></math>. Through each point of the Fano plane lies three lines, so let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{x}=[1,x,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{x}=[1,x,0]</annotation></semantics></math> be another line through <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let the two points of the Fano plane on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(xj,j,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(xj,j,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(xk,k,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(xk,k,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{x}=PQV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{x}=PQV</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining two points <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,N</annotation></semantics></math> of the Fano plane are determine by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=PX\cap QY" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=PX\cap QY</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=PY\cap QX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=PY\cap QX</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,N,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,N,V</annotation></semantics></math> are collinear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of writing <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we can define <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=kh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=kh</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every BM-special Fano plane can be described by the parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b,x,k,h,s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b,x,k,h,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. However this description is not unique, as we may swap <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and the line <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VPQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VPQ</annotation></semantics></math> with the line <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VMN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VMN</annotation></semantics></math>. In any case, the number of such descriptions will be same for each Fano plane.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">(of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 2.6 Contribution of this paper ‣ 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals ‣ A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)
Suppose that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=a^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and put <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=b_{0}+b_{1}e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=b_{0}+b_{1}e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}=b_{0}^{\prime}+b_{1}^{\prime}e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}=b_{0}^{\prime}+b_{1}^{\prime}e</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose there exists a BM-special Fano plane in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,b,x,k,h,s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,b,x,k,h,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we will show that there exists a BM-special Fano plane in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,b^{\prime},x^{\prime},k^{\prime},h,s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,b^{\prime},x^{\prime},k^{\prime},h,s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xk=x^{\prime}k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xk=x^{\prime}k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=xk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=xk</annotation></semantics></math>. We have, with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=j/k\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=j/k\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(\theta h,kh,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\theta h,kh,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(\theta,k,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(\theta,k,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta,k,h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta,k,h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(0,s,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(0,s,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(0,t,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(0,t,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,t\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,t\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=(0,0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=(0,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=PX\cap QY" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=PX\cap QY</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=PY\cap QX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=PY\cap QX</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

